[[:db/retract 17592186179945 :question/answer-fields 17592186177670] {:db/id "q-124871/mathematical-correction-v1/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4b5b1f8-2205-55dd-8481-133a50f9b030" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices [17592186177675 17592186232883 17592186177674 17592186177671 17592186177673] :answer-field/correct 17592186232883 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179945 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2y\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,dy+3z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along $C$, $x(t)=1$, $y(t)=t$, and $z(t)=t^2$, so $x'(t)=0$, $y'(t)=1$, and $z'(t)=2t$. Substitute both the coordinates and their derivatives:\n\n$$\\int_C 2y\\,dx+\\ln x\\,dy+3z\\,dz\n=\\int_1^3\\left(2t\\cdot0+\\ln(1)\\cdot1+3t^2\\cdot2t\\right)\\,dt\n=\\int_1^3 6t^3\\,dt.$$\n\nThus the equivalent integral is $\\int_1^3 6t^3\\,dt$, and its value is $[\\tfrac32t^4]_1^3=120$.\n\nThe original source accepted $\\int_1^3 6t^2\\,dt$ and its worked solution changed $3t^2\\cdot2t$ into $6t^2$. That simplification loses a factor of $t$. The study choice replaces that exponent with $3$; all other original choices are retained." :db/ensure [:question/validate] :question/answer-fields ["q-124871/mathematical-correction-v1/selection"]}]
